I have been thinking about this a bit more, it bugs me a lot.

Crawl opening up has caused the following phenomena:
* players writing patches and bugfixes
* players contributing new vaults (predefined maps that need to come in 
  as large a variety as possible for good effect)
* players discussing changes in-depth, hinting at balance issues

Each of these has problems: we may not like a certain patch (and will not
use it then). A new vault may be buggy or way too hard (dito). Players
confuse "oh my, this godly invocation did kill me - bug! balance broken!!
murder and treason!!!" with proper feedback.

All of these could be muted down by moving into the Ivory Tower. But: we
gain a lot more than what we expend in resources (it definitely is
important to be able to say No! and that often). Players are truly
encouraged to do stuff (we got tiles and telnet servers by enthusiasts in
amazingly short time). And finally, there are players who get more and more
familiar with the code base. This makes devteam changing much more likely
and simpler. 
Crawl was at one point close to dying: Brent Ross, the last active developer
from 4.0, got stuck with serious balance issues in his 4.1 betas. (The game
was not really playable due to gross hardship, even by Crawl standards.)
There was a period of Hibernation (no releases, little public notice of
Brent) for several years. I think this is much more improbable with the
current model.

What does that mean for Nethack (not going to stay off-topic all the time):
the Nethack society is known for having come up with great many patches
(you know the database, I presume). Players discuss stuff over here.
However, has there ever been a sign of exhaustion for lack of positive
feedback? 
I do not claim that Nethack is at a point where Crawl was three or four
years ago - after all, the new version could be released right after the
tournament. But I think that actively making use of fans is a great thing
to do for a free open source project: there is nothing else we have but
fans. It is that sense in which I find the NH DevTeam to be anachrostic and
unfair towards their players (yes, I know by name a few players who will
now jump up and defend that policy).

